#{[17592186532704 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)=2{y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{T}y)=4{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 4y \\\\  & =4\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 16 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-68 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532708 "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=5Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5Ax-b)=5A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & =(5A)^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =10\\cdot {A}^{T}(5Ax-b) \\\\  & =10\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}\\left(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\left(5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 18\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 120 \\\\ 180\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186532929 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (k)))+((2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (3))+((-2)\\cdot (k-1))+((2)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(0)+(2-2k)+(-6k)\\\\ &=2-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-8k&=0\\\\ -8k&=-2\\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"] [17592186532943 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))+((4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-8)))+((-4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-4)-(k)\\cdot (-8)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (12-6k))+((4)\\cdot (-6))+((-4)\\cdot (8k-20))\\\\ &=(12k-24)+(-24)+(80-32k)\\\\ &=32-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32-20k&=0\\\\ -20k&=-32\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{5}.$"] [17592186532947 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 3 & -4 \\\\ k & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((-6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 8 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -3 \\\\ -4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (8)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (7)-(-3)\\cdot (-4)))+((-4)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (8)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))\\\\ &=((-6)\\cdot (45))+((-3)\\cdot (7k-12))+((-4)\\cdot (8k+12))\\\\ &=(-270)+(36-21k)+(-32k-48)\\\\ &=-53k-282\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-53k-282&=0\\\\ -53k&=282\\\\ k&=-\\frac{282}{53}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{282}{53}.$"] [17592186532948 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\