t((-1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (0)))+((1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(1)\\cdot (k)))+((-1)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (0)-(0)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (0))+((1)\\cdot (-k-4))+((-1)\\cdot (0))\\\\ &=(0)+(-k-4)+(0)\\\\ &=-k-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-4&=0\\\\ -k&=4\\\\ k&=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-4.$"] [17592186532955 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & k \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & k \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-1)))+((-2)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-2)-(k)\\cdot (-3)))+((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (-1)-(0)\\cdot (-3)))\\\\ &=((2)\\cdot (k))+((-2)\\cdot (3k-6))+((0)\\cdot (-3))\\\\ &=(2k)+(12-6k)+(0)\\\\ &=12-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4k&=0\\\\ -4k&=-12\\\\ k&=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=3.$"] [17592186532959 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ k & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((1)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((-3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot ((0)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (0)-(-2)\\cdot (-2)))+((-3)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-2)-(0)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((1)\\cdot (-4))+((3)\\cdot (-4))+((-3)\\cdot (-2k))\\\\ &=(-4)+(-12)+(6k)\\\\ &=6k-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6k-16&=0\\\\ 6k&=16\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{3}.$"] [17592186532963 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -8 & 8 & k \\\\ -2 & 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}8 & k \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((3)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & k \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((5)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot ((8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (5)))+((3)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (-8)-(k)\\cdot (-2)))+((5)\\cdot ((-8)\\cdot (5)-(8)\\cdot (-2)))\\\\ &=((3)\\cdot (-5k-64))+((3)\\cdot (2k+64))+((5)\\cdot (-24))\\\\ &=(-15k-192)+(6k+192)+(-120)\\\\ &=-9k-120\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9k-120&=0\\\\ -9k&=120\\\\ k&=-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=-\\frac{40}{3}.$"] [17592186532964 "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 7 \\\\ k & -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=\\left((6)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 7 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((7)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 7 \\\\ k & -3\\end{vmatrix}\\right)+\\left((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ k & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\right)\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot ((-2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (-2)))+((7)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-3)-(7)\\cdot (k)))+((4)\\cdot ((2)\\cdot (-2)-(-2)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((6)\\cdot (20))+((7)\\cdot (-7k-6))+((4)\\cdot (2k-4))\\\\ &=(120)+(-49k-42)+(8k-16)\\\\ &=62-41k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}62-41k&=0\\\\ -41k&=-62\\\\ k&=\\frac{62}{41}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{62}{41}.$"] [17592186533019 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2 uv + u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 v + 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2 uv + u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 v + 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2 uv + u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (2 uv + u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨4 uv + 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 v - 1,\\,1 - 2 u,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(4 uv + 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 v - 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (1 - 2 u)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u + 4 v^{2} + 2 v- 4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(- 4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv + 4 v^{2} + 4 v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533020 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 4,\\,-2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & - v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨(- uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- uv - u - v,\\,2 uv,\\,2 u + 2 v⟩\\cdot ⟨v + 1,\\,u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- uv - u - v)\\cdot (v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (u + 1)+(2 u + 2 v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- uv^{2} - 2 uv - u - v^{2} - v+2 u^{2} v + 2 uv+2 u + 2 v \\\\  & =2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,2 xy,\\,2 x + 2 y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{4}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}(2 u^{2} v - uv^{2} + u - v^{2} + v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533024 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,u^{2} + v^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2 u⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2 v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 u \\\\ 1 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 u \\\\ 0 & 2 v\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=u^{2} + v^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,2 - (u^{2} + v^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨u + 2 v,\\,- u + v,\\,- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2⟩\\cdot ⟨- 2 u,\\,- 2 v,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(u + 2 v)\\cdot (- 2 u)+(- u + v)\\cdot (- 2 v)+(- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 2 u^{2} - 4 uv+2 uv - 2 v^{2}- u^{2} - v^{2} + 2 \\\\  & =- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨x + 2 y,\\,- x + y,\\,2 - z⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A. \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}(- 3 u^{2} - 2 uv - 3 v^{2} + 2)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186533026 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,- 2 uv - u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,- 2 v - 1⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(- 2 uv - u - v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,- 2 u - 1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\left\\langle \\begin{vmatrix}0 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 1 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & - 2 v - 1 \\\\ 0 & - 2 u - 1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\right\\rangle  \\\\  & =⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=- 2 uv - u - v} \\\\  & =⟨2 (- 2 uv - u - v),\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v,\\,2 uv,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨2 v + 1,\\,2 u + 1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(- 4 uv - 2 u - 2 v)\\cdot (2 v + 1)+(2 uv)\\cdot (2 u + 1)+(u + v)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =- 8 uv^{2} - 8 uv - 2 u - 4 v^{2} - 2 v+4 u^{2} v + 2 uv+u + v \\\\  & =4 u^{2} v - 8 uv^{2} - 6 uv - u - 4 v^{2} - v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2 z,\\,2 xy,\\,x + y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v